<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Damrong Rajanubhab</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1862">1862</a>–<a href="/years/1943">1943</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Prince Damrong</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Prince Damrong Rajanubhab was a son of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongkut">Mongkut</a> and the leading administrator of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chulalongkorn">Chulalongkorn</a>'s reforms in Siam. As Minister of the Interior from 1892 to 1915 he built the thesaphiban system, grouping provinces into monthon run by salaried officials in place of hereditary governors. A prolific scholar, he is regarded as the father of modern Thai historiography.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/damrong-rajanubhab.jpg" alt="Damrong Rajanubhab" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ministry of Interior · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tisavarakumara_Damrong_Rajanubhab.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Born Prince Tisavarakumara on 21 June 1862, the son of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongkut">Mongkut</a> and Consort Chum, a lesser royal wife, Damrong learned Thai and Pali from private tutors and English at the Royal School. At 14 he entered a palace school created by his half-brother King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chulalongkorn">Chulalongkorn</a>. He commanded the Royal Guards Regiment from 1880, built army schools and helped modernise the army, and in 1887 became commander-in-chief of the army and Minister of Education in the king's provisional cabinet. In December 1891, on a journey to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, he recruited Gustave Rolin-Jaequemyns as general adviser to the king.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chulalongkorn">Chulalongkorn</a> promoted him over his older half-brothers to head the Ministry of the North in 1892; with the southern and western provinces added in 1894, it became the Ministry of the Interior. Damrong retired older officials in favour of men loyal to him and opened promotion to clerks on merit. His ministry also took over provincial revenue, irrigation, forestry, mines and a provincial gendarmerie. A tour of the northern provinces in 1892 had convinced him that they were almost cut off from the centre.</p><p>Under the thesaphiban system each monthon had commissioners for judicial, financial and administrative affairs. Provincial nobles lost revenues such as judicial fees and transit duties, were paid salaries from them and were often transferred, which cut their local ties. The Ministry of Interior regulations of 1899 made the changes countrywide, and state revenue doubled. Relations with King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vajiravudh">Vajiravudh</a> after 1910 were less close, and Damrong resigned in 1915, officially for his health.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prajadhipok">Prajadhipok</a> he proposed the Royal Institute and became its first president; the National Library and the National Museum grew out of his work. The historian Nithi Aeusrivongse described the Damrong school as joining the royal chronicle to nineteenth-century Western history, and his work is now often seen as overly nationalistic. A defender of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/absolute-monarchy">absolute monarchy</a>, he was exiled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penang">Penang</a> after the Siamese revolution of 1932, returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a> in 1942 and died on 1 December 1943, a day now kept as Damrong Rajanupab Day.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Damrong Rajanubhab” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Prince Damrong</b> Rajanubhab, as Minister of the Interior, replaced hereditary governors with salaried officials grouped in monthon, or circles, under the thesaphiban system, while tributary rulers were turned into provincial nobility.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rattanakosin-kingdom">Rattanakosin Kingdom · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/thailand">Thailand</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/thailand">Thailand</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=13.75,100.52&amp;year=1914" aria-label="Bangkok on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7304 1652 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Damrong Rajanubhab"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7754 1922h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7754 1922h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=13.75,100.52&amp;year=1914">What was here in 1914? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
